diagnosis_text:
Affected component: router1’s access interface toward the pc1 LAN (router1 eth1, dataplane/L2). Mechanism: With router1 eth1 down, the 195.11.14.0/24 segment is effectively severed at the router. pc1’s NIC is up, but it cannot complete ARP to 195.11.14.1 or exchange any L2 frames with the gateway, so pings to the gateway and beyond fail. Meanwhile, the partner AS side (router2–pc2) remains healthy, so pc2 can reach its own gateway normally. Localization: On router1, eth1 shows state DOWN and lacks UP/RUNNING flags (ifconfig eth1: flags=4098<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>; ip addr: “state DOWN”). ethtool on router1 eth1 reports “Link detected: no.” Router1’s eth0 and router2’s interfaces are UP, and BGP/forwarding toward 200.1.1.0/24 are present, isolating the fault to router1 eth1. On pc1, eth0 is UP/LOWER_UP with correct IP (195.11.14.2/24) and default route via 195.11.14.1, but it shows RX packets 0, consistent with the far end being down. Root cause: router1’s eth1 interface toward pc1 is down (no link), breaking the L2 segment for 195.11.14.0/24. Corrective fix: Bring up router1 eth1 and verify physical/virtual cabling. On router1: ip link set dev eth1 up (or ifconfig eth1 up); confirm link (ethtool eth1 shows “Link detected: yes”) and that 195.11.14.1/24 is present, then test pc1 -> 195.11.14.1 and end-to-end reachability. Ensure the interface is auto-up on boot (e.g., persist in startup scripts). 
=== END ===